data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider allowed synchronous LINQ operations via the `AllowSynchronousIO` opt-in (sync-over-async). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** All synchronous query/save operations on the Cosmos provider now throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime — there is no workaround.

### Affected calls in your code:

| Sync (breaks) | Async (replacement) |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Fix — convert to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key points:

1. **Remove** the `AllowSynchronousIO()` / sync-over-async configuration — it no longer compiles.
2. **Async must propagate** up the call stack (`async Task<T>` all the way to the controller).
3. The underlying Cosmos DB SDK is async-only; the old opt-in was always a hack that risked deadlocks — .NET 11 simply removes it.
